{
  "analysis": "I'm starting fresh. I need to reverse-engineer the ./mystery binary to write an equivalent mystery.c. First I should locate the binary and examine its behavior.",
  "plan": "First, find the mystery binary's location and basic info. The working directory is /tmp but the task shows /app. Let me check both. I'll examine the file, run it, capture output, and decompile it to understand its function.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /app 2>/dev/null; ls -la /tmp 2>/dev/null | head\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "find / -name mystery -type f 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Found the mystery binary at /app/mystery (21120 bytes). The patch history references image.ppm and out.txt, suggesting the program manipulates a PPM image. Let me examine the binary's behavior and tooling available.",
  "plan": "I'll check available reverse-engineering tools (objdump, strings, gdb, etc.), examine the binary type, run it with no args to see what it does, and look for strings. This will tell me what inputs it expects (e.g., image.ppm).",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cd /app && file mystery\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "which strings objdump gdb xxd gcc readelf nm python3 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "strings -n 6 mystery | head -80\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The binary is a ray tracer! It has functions: vector_create, vector_normalize, vector_add, vector_subtract, vector_dot, vector_length, ray_create, ray_at, is_in_shadow, write_image, free_image. It writes image.ppm and prints progress. The source was orig.c. This is a classic ray tracer. I need to decompile to reconstruct the exact behavior (scene, lighting, dimensions, output format).",
  "plan": "Run the binary to observe its behavior and output. Check for debug info to recover variables. Disassemble with objdump. I'll start by running it and capturing output, then look at the disassembly of key functions.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "readelf -S mystery | grep -i debug\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "./mystery; echo EXIT=$?\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "It's a ray tracer (functions like ray_create, is_in_shadow, vector operations). It creates an 800x600 image.ppm. No debug info. I need to reconstruct the exact rendering. Let me capture the output image and examine its dimensions, then disassemble to understand the scene and algorithm.",
  "plan": "First, examine the generated image.ppm (header, dimensions, size). Then disassemble the binary focusing on main, write_image, and the render loop to recover exact parameters. I'll dump full disassembly to a file for analysis.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -c 32 image.ppm | od -c | head\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "stat -c '%s' image.ppm; head -2 image.ppm\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "objdump -d -M intel mystery > /tmp/disasm.txt 2>&1; wc -l /tmp/disasm.txt\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Image is ASCII PPM (P3), 800x600, maxval 255, ~5.36MB. No debug info. This is a ray tracer with functions I can identify. I need to decompile to reconstruct the scene and algorithm exactly. Let me examine the disassembly, focusing on function structure and format strings.",
  "plan": "First, list all functions from the disassembly (objdump shows <funcname>:). Then examine write_image to understand the pixel output format, and main to understand scene setup. I'll dump the symbol table too.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "nm mystery | grep -i ' T '\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "grep -E '^[0-9]+ <' /tmp/disasm.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Complete function list obtained. This is a ray tracer with: vector ops (create, add, subtract, multiply, dot, length, normalize), ray_create, ray_at, sphere_intersect, plane_intersect, sky_color, is_in_shadow, trace, allocate_image, write_image, free_image, main. I need to decompile these to reconstruct the exact algorithm. Let me read the disassembly in sections.",
  "plan": "Read the disassembly of main and the math/geometry functions first. I'll view /tmp/disasm.txt sections. Starting with the beginning through the vector functions.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "awk '/<main>:/,/<_fini>:/' /tmp/disasm.txt | head -250\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "This is a ray tracer. I have main plus all subfunctions. I need to reconstruct the exact scene and computation. The vector functions are small and will reveal the vec3 struct layout (likely {float x,y,z} or with padding). Let me decompile the math/geometry helper functions first to understand data structures, then main and the trace logic.",
  "plan": "Dump the vector helper functions and the geometry functions (ray_create, ray_at, sphere_intersect, plane_intersect, sky_color, is_in_shadow, trace, allocate_image, write_image) from the disassembly. Also dump .rodata to read float constants. I'll read these in chunks.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "awk '/<vector_create>:/,/<main>:/' /tmp/disasm.txt > /tmp/vec.txt; sed -n '1,200p' /tmp/vec.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "I now understand the structure: it's a ray tracer with vector/scene functions. I can verify any reimplementation locally by comparing output byte-for-byte against ./mystery. Strategy: reverse-engineer the exact constants and algorithm, write mystery.c, compile, and iterate until image.ppm and stdout match exactly. First I need the .rodata float constants (scene geometry, camera, light) and the logic of trace/is_in_shadow/sky_color/write_image/main.",
  "plan": "Dump .rodata to extract all float constants. Then extract the geometry/trace functions to understand the algorithm. I'll capture a reference output first so I can verify byte-for-byte later.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cp image.ppm /tmp/image.ref.ppm; ./mystery > /tmp/out.ref.txt 2>&1; wc -c /tmp/image.ref.ppm /tmp/out.ref.txt\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "objdump -s -j .rodata mystery > /tmp/rodata.txt; cat /tmp/rodata.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "I have the .rodata constants. Key floats (LE): 0x3004=4.0, 0x3008≈0.001, 0x300c≈tiny, 0x3010=0.2, 0x3014=0.9, 0x3018=0.4, 0x301c=-1.5, 0x3020=0.8, 0x3024=8388608.0, 0x3028≈255.99, 0x302c=-1.0, 0x3030=100.0, 0x3034=600.0, 0x3038=800.0, 0x303c=960.0, 0x3040≈2.6667, 0x3044≈1.3333, 0x3048≈tiny. Vector constants at 0x31a0: (0,0,0,...) and (0,-1,-5,1). The vec3 struct is {x=low, y, z} with 16-byte alignment (the vector functions use addps on xmm0/xmm1 and addss on the scalar for z — actually they operate on 4-wide). Reference image and stdout are saved. I need the exact algorithm. Let me read the remaining functions.",
  "plan": "Dump sphere_intersect (rest), plane_intersect, sky_color, is_in_shadow, trace, write_image, allocate_image, and the remainder of main. I'll read them in chunks to reconstruct the exact formulas.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "awk '/<plane_intersect>:/,/<ray_at>:/' /tmp/disasm.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "I've decoded the data layout: vec3 is a 16-byte struct (x,y,z,w) returned across xmm0:xmm1. Key constants: 0x3008=0.001 (eps), 0x3010=0.2, 0x3014=0.9, 0x3018=0.4, 0x301c=-1.5, 0x3020=0.8, 0x3028=255.99, 0x302c=-1.0, 0x3030=100.0, 0x3034=600.0, 0x3038=800.0, 0x303c=960.0, 0x3040=2.66667, 0x3044=1.33333. sky_color returns (1.0, 0.7-0.3*y, -y). Progress = 100*j/600. I still need the full main, trace, is_in_shadow, and the rest of sphere_intersect to nail the camera/scene/shading. I have reference outputs saved to compare byte-for-byte.",
  "plan": "Dump the full main, trace, and is_in_shadow functions so I can reconstruct the ray/light/scene setup and shading. I'll save them to files and read them.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "awk '/<is_in_shadow>:/,/<sky_color>:/' /tmp/disasm.txt > /tmp/shad.txt; wc -l /tmp/shad.txt; sed -n '1,120p' /tmp/shad.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

I'll continue decoding. Let me get the rest of sphere_intersect and trace.

{
  "analysis": "I've decoded the vector layout (vec3 = {x,y,z} 16-byte aligned), and have constant values. I need the full sphere_intersect, trace, and main to reconstruct scene/shading exactly. I'll rely on byte-diff against the saved reference to verify.",
